\details{
\code{AIC()} computes \eqn{-2 \ell + k p}, where \eqn{\ell} is the fitted
log-likelihood, \eqn{p} is the number of free model parameters, and \eqn{k}
is the non-negative penalty applied to each free parameter. The default
\eqn{k = 2} gives the classical Akaike information criterion.

\code{BIC()} computes \eqn{-2 \ell + \log(n) p}, where \eqn{n} is the total
number of choice occasions, that is, \code{sum(object$choice_data$Tp)}.

\code{coef()} returns the regression coefficients \eqn{b}. Coefficient names are
derived from \code{object$mod$var_names}.

\code{confint()} constructs Wald confidence intervals for \eqn{b}.

\code{logLik()} returns the fitted log-likelihood value stored in \code{object$ll_fit}.
The \code{"df"} attribute is the number of free model parameters,
\code{object$mod$length_theta}, and the \code{"nobs"} attribute is the total number of
choice occasions, \code{sum(object$choice_data$Tp)}.

\code{plot()} visualizes the fitted population distribution of one or two
random effect coefficients selected by \code{parm}.

\code{predict()} computes marginal choice probabilities for each choice occasion.

\code{residuals()} computes response residuals
\eqn{r_{ij} = 1(y_i = j) - \hat{p}_{ij}} for every choice occasion \eqn{i}
and alternative \eqn{j}, where \eqn{\hat{p}_{ij}} is the fitted choice
probability.

\code{summary()} summarizes the model results.

\code{vcov()} returns the covariance matrix of the regression coefficients
\eqn{b}, matching the parameters returned by \code{coef()}.
}
